The tree must be height-balanced. This means that the left and right subtrees should have almost the same height.

I used recursion to build the tree.
First, I find the middle element of the array. I use it as the root. Then I repeat the same process:
Elements on the left become the left subtree. Elements on the right become the right subtree. I find the middle element of each part and create new nodes.

I stop when there are no elements left.
The main challenge was understanding how to choose the root.
If I always choose the first element, the tree can become unbalanced.
I learned that choosing the middle element helps create a balanced tree.
Another challenge was understanding the recursive calls. Each call works with a smaller part of the array.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Every element from the array is used once to create a tree node.
If there are n elements, the algorithm creates n nodes.
Space Complexity: O(log n)
The recursion stack uses space depending on the height of the tree.
Because the tree is balanced, its height is approximately log n.
The output tree itself also takes O(n) space, but this is not included in the auxiliary space complexity.

I use the middle element to create a balanced tree.
The time complexity is O(n), which is optimal because every element must be used.
There is no need to visit the array more than once.
